WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:05.430
Last month, both Richard Branson and Jeff
Bezos made history by traveling to space on

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:07.480
their own private vehicles.

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:11.620
Many have argued that these two didn’t actually
reach “real space” as they didn’t reach

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:14.720
orbit or even travel very far into space.

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:18.930
Though their achievements are extremely impressive
in their own right, I can see why many wouldn’t

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:21.870
exactly consider them astronauts.

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:26.250
Elon Musk, however, is easily capable of ticking
off all the boxes of being an astronaut.

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:30.930
He’s easily capable of using Falcon 9 to
not only cross the Karman line but reach the

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:35.309
ISS, stay at the ISS for several months, and
then return safely.

00:00:35.309 --> 00:00:37.829
Despite this though, Elon has yet to make
the journey.

00:00:37.829 --> 00:00:42.600
So, here’s why Elon refuses to go to space.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:46.750
Before we jump into the video, I wanted to
address that Elon will likely go to space

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:48.350
eventually.

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:54.030
Whether that’s next month or 10 years from
now, I don’t know, but it is likely an eventuality.

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:58.989
So, I wanted to make it clear that I’m not
saying that Elon will never go to space.

00:00:58.989 --> 00:01:03.789
Rather, I simply wanted to take a look at
why he has refused the opportunity thus far

00:01:03.789 --> 00:01:06.540
despite having the technology for years.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:12.230
With that being said, let’s jump into the
first and most obvious reason which is death.

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:17.000
Space travel is not the safest journey to
embark on, and each space flight carries significant

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.120
risk for straight up death.

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:22.950
Even with a Falcon 9, which has proven to
be one of the most reliable rockets of all

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:26.320
time, the risk of death is not negligible.

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:30.360
Nasa estimates that there's a 1 in 60 chance
that a Falcon 9 mission fails but doesn’t

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:31.990
lead to a fatality.

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:37.270
And a 1 in 276 chance that a Falcon 9 mission
fails and leads to a fatality.

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:39.950
That’s about the same chance as having twins.

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:44.940
Elon Musk already has twins and triplets,
so maybe that’s a sign that he shouldn’t

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:45.940
take the trip.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:50.880
But, putting cosmic signs aside, death itself
is not the real challenge for Elon.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.960
The real challenge would be the consequences
that follow his death.

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:57.470
SpaceX and Tesla are by no means mature companies.

00:01:57.470 --> 00:02:02.120
Tesla has recently made strides forward, and
is likely at a point where it can survive

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:04.530
and even thrive without Elon.

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:09.879
But just 3 years ago, Tesla was within single
digit weeks of bankruptcy, and Tesla may have

00:02:09.879 --> 00:02:12.340
gotten wiped out without Elon.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:15.849
Similarly, SpaceX doesn’t really have a
successor lined up.

00:02:15.849 --> 00:02:21.569
Gwynne Shotwell is amazing, and could probably
run the company as well as Elon, but Gwynne

00:02:21.569 --> 00:02:27.329
is actually older than Elon at 57, so she’s
not really a long term successor.

00:02:27.329 --> 00:02:31.120
Considering the current states of SpaceX and
Tesla, it doesn’t make sense for Elon to

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:34.890
risk the futures of these companies, which
is basically his life's work, on a joyride

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:36.239
to space.

00:02:36.239 --> 00:02:40.989
But wait a minute, if SpaceX itself is not
a mature company, there’s no way that Blue

00:02:40.989 --> 00:02:44.150
Origin and Virgin Galactic are mature companies.

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:48.620
So, why were Jeff Bezos and Richard Branson
willing to risk the futures of their space

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:49.620
companies?

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:51.810
Well, the answer is to play catch up.

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:55.670
Virgin Galactic’s SpaceShipTwo has only
had a total of 4 space flights throughout

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:58.310
its lifetime and Richard Branson rode on the
fourth launch.

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:03.359
Fortunately, all of these space flights have
been successful, but with only 4 total flights,

00:03:03.359 --> 00:03:07.370
you wouldn’t exactly describe SpaceShipTwo
as having a stellar track history.

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:12.180
Blue Origin does have a lot more data as they
launched New Shepard a total of 15 times before

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:13.900
Jeff took his flight.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:18.409
Throughout the 15 launches, New Shepard had
14 successful flights and 1 partially successful

00:03:18.409 --> 00:03:19.540
flight.

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:24.159
Even in the partially successful flight, Blue
Origin was able to recover the capsule successfully,

00:03:24.159 --> 00:03:27.329
so New Shepard has a pretty strong safety
record.

00:03:27.329 --> 00:03:31.260
But even that holds no water in comparison
to Falcon 9.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:35.569
The Demo 2 mission we saw last year with Bob
and Doug was literally the 85th launch of

00:03:35.569 --> 00:03:36.569
the Falcon 9.

00:03:36.569 --> 00:03:41.739
It would take years for Virgin Galactic and
Blue Origin to rival SpaceX’s track record.

00:03:41.739 --> 00:03:45.379
At Blue Origin’s current pace of about 1
launch every three months, it would take them

00:03:45.379 --> 00:03:48.590
17.5 years to complete 85 launches.

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:52.310
Even if Blue Origin sped up to SpaceX’s
pace and did a launch every other week with

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:57.129
New Shepard, it would take Blue Origin another
2.5 years to complete 85 launches.

00:03:57.129 --> 00:04:02.989
So, clearly, Virgin Galactic and Blue Origin
needed to catch up in terms of reliability,

00:04:02.989 --> 00:04:07.139
and one of the boldest ways to vouch for the
reliability of your spacecraft is to ride

00:04:07.139 --> 00:04:08.550
it yourself.

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:10.469
And that’s precisely what they did.

00:04:10.469 --> 00:04:15.909
As for Elon, he doesn’t need to vouch for
the reliability of Falcon 9 and he doesn’t

00:04:15.909 --> 00:04:17.370
need to catch up to anyone.

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:22.980
So, Elon riding on Falcon 9 would do little
for SpaceX's future or track record.

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:26.170
Something else we have to consider is that
SpaceX has a completely different customer

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:29.630
base than Blue Origin and Virgin Galactic.

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:32.760
SpaceX doesn’t really care about the space
tourism market.

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:36.840
Everytime I say this, I get comments asking
me if I’ve ever heard about SpaceX’s dearMoon

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:40.400
project or the upcoming Axiom trip in 2022.

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:45.270
The answer is yes, I have heard about these
missions and SpaceX does do tourism trips

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:46.750
here and there.

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:49.570
But this is by no means their focus.

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:53.370
The dearMoon project is basically just a fundraiser
for Starship.

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:57.300
And with the Axiom trip to the ISS, Axiom
is the one that’s handling the tourism side

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:58.540
of the business.

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:01.390
For SpaceX, it’s just another crew mission
to the ISS.

00:05:01.390 --> 00:05:06.430
They’re not spending time or effort in advertising
the service and selling tickets; they’re

00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:08.500
just the vehicle provider.

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:13.550
SpaceX really only deals with commercial customers
and 95% of their missions are business or

00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:14.550
science related.

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:19.280
So, I think it’s safe to say that SpaceX
does not focus on space tourism.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:23.550
For Blue Origin and Virgin Galactic, however,
space tourism is a focal point.

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:27.600
They don’t really have many commercial customers,
and they expect to make the vast majority

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:30.240
of their money by selling tickets to space.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:34.350
Blue Origin, for instance, has apparently
already sold nearly $100 million worth of

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:35.550
space tickets.

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:39.420
This vastly different customer base leads
to vastly different expectations.

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:44.290
Neither NASA nor the US military give a crap
about whether Elon rode on Falcon 9.

00:05:44.290 --> 00:05:47.910
They each have thousands of scientists working
for them, and they’ll perform their own

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:52.820
risk analysis of Falcon 9 to determine whether
they want to contract out a job to SpaceX

00:05:52.820 --> 00:05:53.910
or not.

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:58.360
Similarly, when companies decide on whether
to use a SpaceX rocket, they only care about

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:02.940
three things: payload capacity, price, and
the risk of failure.

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:06.630
Again they don’t give a crap about Elon
riding a rocket.

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:09.930
The space tourism customer base, however,
values this much more.

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:14.880
Here’s the thing, let’s say you’re a
multi millionaire worth about $5 million and

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:19.060
you’re considering buying a $250,000 ticket
to space with either Blue Origin or Virgin

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:20.200
Galactic.

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:24.770
You can look at the track record of New Shepard
and SpaceShipTwo, but you don’t actually

00:06:24.770 --> 00:06:29.170
know the physics behind each spacecraft and
the safety of the two seem pretty similar.

00:06:29.170 --> 00:06:34.160
Now, let’s say Richard Branson rides SpaceShipTwo
to space but Jeff Bezos doesn’t ride New

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:35.160
Shepard.

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:39.170
I don’t know about you, but if I was in
this position, I would definitely choose Virgin

00:06:39.170 --> 00:06:41.240
Galactic over Blue Origin.

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:45.940
The reasoning is that Richard Branson is much
more important than me, and if he’s willing

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:51.760
to trust his life on SpaceShipTwo, then he’s
probably pretty darn confident in its safety.

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:55.710
As you can see, founder’s riding their rocket
holds much more weight within the space tourism

00:06:55.710 --> 00:06:58.430
industry than the commercial space industry.

00:06:58.430 --> 00:07:01.890
And this just further disincentivizes Elon
from making the trip.

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:06.050
Moving on, another factor hindering Elon is
likely the time commitment.

00:07:06.050 --> 00:07:09.910
Blue Origin and Virgin Galactic’s offerings
are specifically designed to be as easy to

00:07:09.910 --> 00:07:11.620
use as possible.

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:16.060
This means minimal training, minimal learning,
and just overall minimal effort.

00:07:16.060 --> 00:07:19.840
And this makes sense considering that the
entire trip is only a couple of minutes.

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:23.880
If Elon were to ride Falcon 9 though, the
training would be much more extensive as in

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:25.080
many months.

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:29.690
The Axiom 1 crew, for instance, will undergo
15 weeks of training before traveling to the

00:07:29.690 --> 00:07:30.690
ISS.

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:34.420
In the businessworld, 15 weeks is more than
an entire fiscal quarter.

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:38.900
Imagine if Elon stepped back from Tesla for
a full quarter to go train for a Falcon 9

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:39.900
flight.

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:43.820
I don’t think Tesla investors, especially
institutional investors and Wall Street would

00:07:43.820 --> 00:07:45.600
be very happy.

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:50.030
And finally, the last reason that Elon is
hesitant in taking a trip to space is that

00:07:50.030 --> 00:07:52.770
maybe he’s saving the journey for something
much larger.

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:56.360
As Jeff Bezos said, it’s not like any of
these guys are actually the first people to

00:07:56.360 --> 00:07:57.850
go to space.

00:07:57.850 --> 00:08:01.990
At this point, people have been traveling
to space for over 50 years.

00:08:01.990 --> 00:08:05.400
And though less than a thousand people have
traveled to space in that time period, would

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:09.790
people even care about Jeff Bezos or Elon
Musk going to space if it wasn’t for their

00:08:09.790 --> 00:08:10.790
popularity.

00:08:10.790 --> 00:08:13.790
I mean just look at SpaceX’s own crewed
missions.

00:08:13.790 --> 00:08:18.120
Everyone knows that Bob and Doug were the
first ones to ride Falcon 9 to the ISS.

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:22.110
But, do you know the astronauts that were
part of Crew 1 or Crew 2?

00:08:22.110 --> 00:08:26.010
Considering this, would an Elon trip actually
be a massive achievement or would it just

00:08:26.010 --> 00:08:28.190
be a big thing cause Elon is involved.

00:08:28.190 --> 00:08:30.280
I think it's more of the latter.

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:35.180
So maybe, Elon doesn’t want to go to space
until the trip itself is historic whether

00:08:35.180 --> 00:08:37.770
that’s a trip around the moon or a trip
to mars.

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:41.729
Speaking of a trip around the moon, a reporter
actually asked Elon if he would be willing

00:08:41.729 --> 00:08:44.850
to join the japanese billionaire on the dearMoon
project.

00:08:44.850 --> 00:09:28.690
And this is how he responded.

00:09:28.690 --> 00:09:33.160
As you can see, Elon was super hesitant in
answering the question and he basically just

00:09:33.160 --> 00:09:36.910
avoided the question and talked about how
Yusaku restored his faith in humanity.

00:09:36.910 --> 00:09:46.660
Elon would eventually answer the question
though and 

00:09:46.660 --> 00:10:03.700
he said.

00:10:03.700 --> 00:10:08.290
As you can see, Elon didn’t technically
refuse to go, but his hestitance makes it

00:10:08.290 --> 00:10:12.820
seem like it’s quite unlikely due to all
the reasons we already outlined in this video.

00:10:12.820 --> 00:10:27.770
With that being said though, Elon has also
stated that he would be down to die 

00:10:27.770 --> 00:10:30.070
on Mars.

00:10:30.070 --> 00:10:35.300
So, maybe that’s been his master plan all
along.

